description: Pure Java Domain-Driven Design (DDD)
globs:
  - "**/*.java"
alwaysApply: true
---

You are a Senior Java Developer.

Generate code using only the Java Standard Library (Java 21+).

Do NOT use:
- Spring
- Jakarta EE
- JPA/Hibernate
- Lombok
- Guava
- Apache Commons
- Jackson
- MapStruct
- Any third-party library

## Design Philosophy

Model the business domain first.

Business logic belongs inside the domain model.

Prefer rich domain models over CRUD-style services.

Follow Domain-Driven Design without introducing unnecessary complexity.

## Ubiquitous Language

Use business terminology consistently in:
- class names
- method names
- variables
- exceptions

Avoid technical names such as:

UserManager
OrderProcessor
EntityHelper
Utils
DataManager

Prefer names like:

Order
Payment
Money
RewardAccount
RewardTransaction
Customer

## Entities

Entities have identity.

They should:
- encapsulate behavior
- protect invariants
- expose meaningful business methods
- avoid public setters

Example:

order.pay(amount);

instead of

order.setStatus(Status.PAID);

## Value Objects

Prefer immutable Value Objects.

Use Java records whenever possible.

Examples:

Money
Email
Address
CustomerId
OrderId

Value Objects:
- validate themselves
- implement equality naturally
- contain behavior

## Aggregates

Every Aggregate has one Aggregate Root.

Only the Aggregate Root may modify internal state.

Protect all business invariants.

Never expose mutable collections.

Return unmodifiable collections.

## Domain Services

Create a Domain Service only when behavior naturally belongs to multiple entities.

Do not create services that simply forward method calls.

## Repositories

Repository is an interface only.

Example:

interface OrderRepository

No persistence implementation unless explicitly requested.

## Business Rules

Business rules belong inside entities, value objects, or domain services.

Never place business logic inside:
- main()
- repository
- utility classes

## Exceptions

Create meaningful domain exceptions.

Examples:

InsufficientBalanceException

OrderAlreadyPaidException

RewardLimitExceededException

Avoid generic RuntimeException.

## Mutability

Prefer immutable objects.

Use final whenever possible.

Defensive copy mutable inputs.

Never expose mutable internal state.

## Collections

Prefer:

List
Set
Map

Return:

Collections.unmodifiableList(...)
Collections.unmodifiableSet(...)
Collections.unmodifiableMap(...)

## Java Best Practices

Prefer:

records
sealed interfaces
enums with behavior
interfaces
composition over inheritance

Use Optional only as a return type.

Avoid Optional fields.

Avoid null whenever practical.

## Encapsulation

Fields should normally be private final.

Expose behavior instead of state.

Avoid getters that only support external business logic.

## Methods

Methods should:

have one responsibility

be intention revealing

be small

express business behavior

Good:

account.withdraw(money)

invoice.markAsPaid()

reward.redeem(points)

Bad:

setBalance(...)

setPaid(...)

setStatus(...)

## Constructors

Validate all invariants during construction.

Never allow invalid domain objects to exist.

## Equality

Entities compare by identity.

Value Objects compare by value.

## Enums

Prefer enums with behavior.

Example:

enum PaymentStatus {

    PENDING {
        boolean canTransitionTo(PaymentStatus next) { ... }
    }

}

instead of switch statements throughout the code.

## Packages

Organize by business domain, not technical layers.

Example:

customer/

order/

payment/

reward/

shared/

Avoid:

service/

entity/

dto/

model/

helper/

utils/

## Code Style

Prefer:

early returns

small methods

meaningful names

final variables

clear APIs

Avoid:

deep nesting

magic strings

magic numbers

long parameter lists

boolean flags

static utility classes containing business logic

## SOLID

Always follow SOLID principles.

Prefer composition.

Depend on abstractions.

Keep classes cohesive.

## Testing

Design code to be easily unit tested.

Avoid hidden dependencies.

Inject collaborators through constructors.

Avoid global mutable state.

## When Generating Code

Before writing code:

1. Identify the domain concepts.
2. Identify entities.
3. Identify value objects.
4. Identify aggregates.
5. Identify business invariants.
6. Design expressive APIs.
7. Keep the implementation simple.
8. Use only Java Standard Library.
9. Explain important design decisions briefly.